            },
            {
              "id": "k8s.pod.host_namespace",
              "name": "K8sPodHostNamespace",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Kubernetes pod that shares the node's network, PID or IPC namespace"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "hostNetwork: true puts the pod on the node's network (it can reach node-local services and sniff traffic); hostPID: true lets it see and signal every process on the node; hostIPC: true shares the node's shared memory. Each removes an isolation boundary."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Remove hostNetwork, hostPID and hostIPC. Expose the pod through a Service, and use a dedicated node agent for node-level work.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** hostNetwork: true puts the pod on the node's network (it can reach node-local services and sniff traffic); hostPID: true lets it see and signal every process on the node; hostIPC: true shares the node's shared memory. Each removes an isolation boundary.\n\n**Fix:** Remove hostNetwork, hostPID and hostIPC. Expose the pod through a Service, and use a dedicated node agent for node-level work."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-668"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "php.exec.command",
              "name": "PhpExecCommand",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Command run with a value built from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "system(), exec(), shell_exec(), passthru(), popen(), proc_open(), pcntl_exec() and a backtick command pass their string to a shell, so a request value in it can add ';', '&&' or '$(...)' and run other commands. The finding appears when $_GET, $_POST, $_REQUEST, $_COOKIE, php://input or a request object reaches the call inside one function (with a trace). A value that went through escapeshellarg(), escapeshellcmd(), a numeric cast or a hash is not reported, and neither is proc_open() with an argument array."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Avoid the shell: call the program with fixed arguments, use a PHP function for the job (mkdir, copy, ZipArchive), or pass proc_open() an argument array. When a shell is unavoidable, wrap each value in escapeshellarg() and validate it against an allowlist first.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** system(), exec(), shell_exec(), passthru(), popen(), proc_open(), pcntl_exec() and a backtick command pass their string to a shell, so a request value in it can add ';', '&&' or '$(...)' and run other commands. The finding appears when $_GET, $_POST, $_REQUEST, $_COOKIE, php://input or a request object reaches the call inside one function (with a trace). A value that went through escapeshellarg(), escapeshellcmd(), a numeric cast or a hash is not reported, and neither is proc_open() with an argument array.\n\n**Fix:** Avoid the shell: call the program with fixed arguments, use a PHP function for the job (mkdir, copy, ZipArchive), or pass proc_open() an argument array. When a shell is unavoidable, wrap each value in escapeshellarg() and validate it against an allowlist first."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "php.include.dynamic",
              "name": "PhpIncludeDynamic",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "include or require of a path built from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "include and require run the file they name as PHP. If a request value chooses the path, an attacker can load a file of their choosing: a local file such as an uploaded image or a log that holds injected PHP, or, when allow_url_include is on, a remote script. A value that went through basename() or a numeric cast is not reported."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Map a request value to a file with an allowlist (an array of page names to paths) and include only the mapped path. Never pass a request value, even with a suffix added, to include or require.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** include and require run the file they name as PHP. If a request value chooses the path, an attacker can load a file of their choosing: a local file such as an uploaded image or a log that holds injected PHP, or, when allow_url_include is on, a remote script. A value that went through basename() or a numeric cast is not reported.\n\n**Fix:** Map a request value to a file with an allowlist (an array of page names to paths) and include only the mapped path. Never pass a request value, even with a suffix added, to include or require."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-98"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "php.sql.injection",
              "name": "PhpSqlInjection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL text built from request data (or built from a value that is not a literal)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "mysqli_query(), pg_query() and the other query functions, ->query(), ->exec(), ->prepare(), WordPress's $wpdb->query() and get_*() methods, Laravel's DB::raw(), DB::select() and whereRaw()/selectRaw()/orderByRaw() run the SQL text they are given. A request value placed in that text can close a string and change the query. The finding is high when a request value reaches the call inside one function (with a trace) and medium with low confidence when the text is only built by concatenation or interpolation from a value that is not a literal in the strict sense: a call result or a variable assigned in the function, not a parameter, a table or column name, or a variable passed alone. Values that went through mysqli_real_escape_string(), addslashes(), ->quote(), esc_sql(), a numeric cast or a hash are not reported, and a $wpdb->prepare() call is not text built by the caller."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use placeholders and pass the values separately: $pdo->prepare('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?') then ->execute([$id]), mysqli_stmt_bind_param(), $wpdb->prepare('... WHERE id = %d', $id), or Laravel's where('id', $id) and whereRaw('id = ?', [$id]). Names of tables and columns cannot be bound, so check them against an allowlist.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** mysqli_query(), pg_query() and the other query functions, ->query(), ->exec(), ->prepare(), WordPress's $wpdb->query() and get_*() methods, Laravel's DB::raw(), DB::select() and whereRaw()/selectRaw()/orderByRaw() run the SQL text they are given. A request value placed in that text can close a string and change the query. The finding is high when a request value reaches the call inside one function (with a trace) and medium with low confidence when the text is only built by concatenation or interpolation from a value that is not a literal in the strict sense: a call result or a variable assigned in the function, not a parameter, a table or column name, or a variable passed alone. Values that went through mysqli_real_escape_string(), addslashes(), ->quote(), esc_sql(), a numeric cast or a hash are not reported, and a $wpdb->prepare() call is not text built by the caller.\n\n**Fix:** Use placeholders and pass the values separately: $pdo->prepare('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?') then ->execute([$id]), mysqli_stmt_bind_param(), $wpdb->prepare('... WHERE id = %d', $id), or Laravel's where('id', $id) and whereRaw('id = ?', [$id]). Names of tables and columns cannot be bound, so check them against an allowlist."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "php.xss.echo",
              "name": "PhpXssEcho",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "echo or print of request data without escaping"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "echo, print, <?= ?>, printf() and die() write their argument into the response. A request value in it, such as $_GET['q'] or $_SERVER['PHP_SELF'], becomes markup the browser runs as script (reflected cross-site scripting). A value that went through htmlspecialchars(), htmlentities(), esc_html(), esc_attr(), e(), strip_tags(), json_encode() or a numeric cast is not reported."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Escape for the context where the value lands: htmlspecialchars($value, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8') for HTML text and attributes (esc_html()/esc_attr() in WordPress, Blade's escaped echo, Twig's autoescaping), and a JSON or URL encoder inside script blocks and URLs. For $_SERVER['PHP_SELF'] in a form action, use an empty action or htmlspecialchars().",
                "markdown": "**Why:** echo, print, <?= ?>, printf() and die() write their argument into the response. A request value in it, such as $_GET['q'] or $_SERVER['PHP_SELF'], becomes markup the browser runs as script (reflected cross-site scripting). A value that went through htmlspecialchars(), htmlentities(), esc_html(), esc_attr(), e(), strip_tags(), json_encode() or a numeric cast is not reported.\n\n**Fix:** Escape for the context where the value lands: htmlspecialchars($value, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8') for HTML text and attributes (esc_html()/esc_attr() in WordPress, Blade's escaped echo, Twig's autoescaping), and a JSON or URL encoder inside script blocks and URLs. For $_SERVER['PHP_SELF'] in a form action, use an empty action or htmlspecialchars()."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "php.ssrf.request",
              "name": "PhpSsrfRequest",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Server-side request to a URL built from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "curl_init(), curl_setopt(CURLOPT_URL), get_headers(), fsockopen(), wp_remote_get() and friends, and file_get_contents(), fopen(), readfile() and file() when the whole location comes from the request, make the server fetch what the request names. An attacker can point it at internal services, cloud metadata addresses or local files (file://)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Fetch only URLs you build from a fixed scheme and host plus an encoded value, or check the parsed host against an allowlist (and resolve it to a public address) before the request. Turn off redirects, restrict CURLOPT_PROTOCOLS to HTTP(S), and in WordPress use wp_safe_remote_get().",
                "markdown": "**Why:** curl_init(), curl_setopt(CURLOPT_URL), get_headers(), fsockopen(), wp_remote_get() and friends, and file_get_contents(), fopen(), readfile() and file() when the whole location comes from the request, make the server fetch what the request names. An attacker can point it at internal services, cloud metadata addresses or local files (file://).\n\n**Fix:** Fetch only URLs you build from a fixed scheme and host plus an encoded value, or check the parsed host against an allowlist (and resolve it to a public address) before the request. Turn off redirects, restrict CURLOPT_PROTOCOLS to HTTP(S), and in WordPress use wp_safe_remote_get()."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "php.path.traversal",
              "name": "PhpPathTraversal",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "File path built from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "fopen(), file_get_contents(), readfile(), file(), file_put_contents(), unlink(), rename(), mkdir(), scandir(), move_uploaded_file() and similar functions use the path they are given. A request value containing '../' (or the name of an uploaded file) reaches outside the intended directory and reads, overwrites or deletes other files. A value that went through basename() or a numeric cast is not reported."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Take the base name only (basename()), or map an id to a stored file name. Join it to a fixed directory, resolve with realpath() and check the result starts with the directory's real path before using it. Generate names for uploads yourself.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** fopen(), file_get_contents(), readfile(), file(), file_put_contents(), unlink(), rename(), mkdir(), scandir(), move_uploaded_file() and similar functions use the path they are given. A request value containing '../' (or the name of an uploaded file) reaches outside the intended directory and reads, overwrites or deletes other files. A value that went through basename() or a numeric cast is not reported.\n\n**Fix:** Take the base name only (basename()), or map an id to a stored file name. Join it to a fixed directory, resolve with realpath() and check the result starts with the directory's real path before using it. Generate names for uploads yourself."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "php.extract.request",
              "name": "PhpExtractRequest",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "extract() of request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "extract() creates a local variable for every key of the array it is given. With $_GET, $_POST, $_REQUEST, $_COOKIE or a request array, an attacker chooses variable names and so can overwrite variables the code relies on, such as $is_admin or a file path, which opens the way to bypassed checks and file inclusion. EXTR_PREFIX_ALL is not reported."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Read the keys you need one by one ($name = $_POST['name'] ?? ''), or pass EXTR_PREFIX_ALL with a prefix so the new names cannot collide with your own.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** extract() creates a local variable for every key of the array it is given. With $_GET, $_POST, $_REQUEST, $_COOKIE or a request array, an attacker chooses variable names and so can overwrite variables the code relies on, such as $is_admin or a file path, which opens the way to bypassed checks and file inclusion. EXTR_PREFIX_ALL is not reported.\n\n**Fix:** Read the keys you need one by one ($name = $_POST['name'] ?? ''), or pass EXTR_PREFIX_ALL with a prefix so the new names cannot collide with your own."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-621"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "php.crypto.weak_password_hash",
              "name": "PhpCryptoWeakPasswordHash",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "md5() or sha1() of a password"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "md5() and sha1() are fast general-purpose hashes: an attacker who gets the stored values tries billions of guesses a second on a GPU. The finding fires when the argument names a password ($password, $_POST['pass'], $user_pwd and the like)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hash passwords with password_hash($password, PASSWORD_DEFAULT) (or PASSWORD_ARGON2ID) and check them with password_verify(); rehash on login with password_needs_rehash().",
                "markdown": "**Why:** md5() and sha1() are fast general-purpose hashes: an attacker who gets the stored values tries billions of guesses a second on a GPU. The finding fires when the argument names a password ($password, $_POST['pass'], $user_pwd and the like).\n\n**Fix:** Hash passwords with password_hash($password, PASSWORD_DEFAULT) (or PASSWORD_ARGON2ID) and check them with password_verify(); rehash on login with password_needs_rehash()."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-916"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "php.blade.unescaped",
              "name": "PhpBladeUnescaped",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Blade {!! !!} of a value that is not a literal"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Blade's {!! !!} prints a value without HTML escaping, where the default double-brace echo escapes it. If the value holds user input, markup in it runs as script. Calls to Laravel helpers that return safe markup (csrf_field(), route(), json_encode(), e() ...) are not reported. The finding has the rule's severity and high confidence when the expression reads the request ($request, request(), old()), and low severity and confidence otherwise."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use Blade's escaped echo (the value inside double curly braces), which escapes. Keep {!! !!} for markup your own code built, and sanitize user-supplied HTML with a purifier (for example HTMLPurifier) before printing it.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Blade's {!! !!} prints a value without HTML escaping, where the default double-brace echo escapes it. If the value holds user input, markup in it runs as script. Calls to Laravel helpers that return safe markup (csrf_field(), route(), json_encode(), e() ...) are not reported. The finding has the rule's severity and high confidence when the expression reads the request ($request, request(), old()), and low severity and confidence otherwise.\n\n**Fix:** Use Blade's escaped echo (the value inside double curly braces), which escapes. Keep {!! !!} for markup your own code built, and sanitize user-supplied HTML with a purifier (for example HTMLPurifier) before printing it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "low",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sql.grant.all",
              "name": "SqlGrantAll",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "GRANT ALL in a SQL file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "GRANT ALL [PRIVILEGES] gives an account every privilege on its target, including the ability to change schema, delete data and, on *.*, administer the server. An application account that is compromised then has all of it. The finding is high for GRANT ALL ON *.* or TO PUBLIC, medium for a whole database, schema or ALL TABLES, and low for a single object."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Grant the privileges the application needs (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) on the specific tables or schema, and keep a separate owner or migration account for DDL.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** GRANT ALL [PRIVILEGES] gives an account every privilege on its target, including the ability to change schema, delete data and, on *.*, administer the server. An application account that is compromised then has all of it. The finding is high for GRANT ALL ON *.* or TO PUBLIC, medium for a whole database, schema or ALL TABLES, and low for a single object.\n\n**Fix:** Grant the privileges the application needs (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) on the specific tables or schema, and keep a separate owner or migration account for DDL."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-250"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sql.credentials.plaintext",
              "name": "SqlCredentialsPlaintext",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Plaintext account password in a SQL file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "CREATE USER ... IDENTIFIED BY 'secret', CREATE ROLE ... PASSWORD 'secret', ALTER USER ... PASSWORD 'secret' and SET PASSWORD = 'secret' store a literal password in a file that lives in version control and is copied around. Values that look like placeholders (<...>, ${...}, %s) and already hashed values (md5..., SCRAM-SHA-256$..., IDENTIFIED BY PASSWORD '*hash') are not reported."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Keep the password out of the file: create the account from a deployment script that reads it from a secret store or an environment variable, or supply it at run time (psql -v and :'password'). Rotate any password that was committed.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** CREATE USER ... IDENTIFIED BY 'secret', CREATE ROLE ... PASSWORD 'secret', ALTER USER ... PASSWORD 'secret' and SET PASSWORD = 'secret' store a literal password in a file that lives in version control and is copied around. Values that look like placeholders (<...>, ${...}, %s) and already hashed values (md5..., SCRAM-SHA-256$..., IDENTIFIED BY PASSWORD '*hash') are not reported.\n\n**Fix:** Keep the password out of the file: create the account from a deployment script that reads it from a secret store or an environment variable, or supply it at run time (psql -v and :'password'). Rotate any password that was committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "sql.function.security_definer_path",
              "name": "SqlFunctionSecurityDefinerPath",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "PostgreSQL SECURITY DEFINER function without a fixed search_path"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A SECURITY DEFINER function runs with its owner's privileges. Without SET search_path, names it uses are resolved through the caller's search_path, so a caller can create a table, function or operator of the same name in a schema searched first and make the function run it with the owner's rights."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Add SET search_path = pg_catalog, public (or just the schemas the function needs, with pg_temp last) to the function definition, and schema-qualify the objects it uses.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** A SECURITY DEFINER function runs with its owner's privileges. Without SET search_path, names it uses are resolved through the caller's search_path, so a caller can create a table, function or operator of the same name in a schema searched first and make the function run it with the owner's rights.\n\n**Fix:** Add SET search_path = pg_catalog, public (or just the schemas the function needs, with pg_temp last) to the function definition, and schema-qualify the objects it uses."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-427"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "go.exec.command",
              "name": "GoExecCommand",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "exec.Command with a program or arguments from request data"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "exec.Command runs the program it names with the arguments it is given. If a request value chooses the program, or reaches the script of 'sh -c', an attacker runs commands of their choosing; as an ordinary argument it can still add options such as '--output=/etc/...' (argument injection). The finding appears when a value of an *http.Request (FormValue, URL, Header, Body ...), a gin, echo, fiber, chi or Gitea-style context, or mux.Vars reaches exec.Command, exec.CommandContext, syscall.Exec or os.StartProcess inside one function (with a trace). A value that went through strconv.Atoi or filepath.Base is not reported."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Run a fixed program with fixed options and pass request values only as separate arguments after '--', checked against an allowlist. Never pass a request value to 'sh -c'; when a shell is unavoidable, map the value to a fixed string.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** exec.Command runs the program it names with the arguments it is given. If a request value chooses the program, or reaches the script of 'sh -c', an attacker runs commands of their choosing; as an ordinary argument it can still add options such as '--output=/etc/...' (argument injection). The finding appears when a value of an *http.Request (FormValue, URL, Header, Body ...), a gin, echo, fiber, chi or Gitea-style context, or mux.Vars reaches exec.Command, exec.CommandContext, syscall.Exec or os.StartProcess inside one function (with a trace). A value that went through strconv.Atoi or filepath.Base is not reported.\n\n**Fix:** Run a fixed program with fixed options and pass request values only as separate arguments after '--', checked against an allowlist. Never pass a request value to 'sh -c'; when a shell is unavoidable, map the value to a fixed string."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "go.sql.injection",
              "name": "GoSqlInjection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL text built from request data (or built from a value that is not a literal)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "database/sql's Query, QueryRow, Exec and Prepare (and their Context forms), sqlx's Queryx, MustExec and NamedExec, and GORM's Raw, Exec, Where, Order, Group, Having and Joins run the SQL text they are given. A request value placed in that text can close a string and change the query. The finding is high when request data reaches the call inside one function (with a trace) and medium with low confidence when the text is built with + or fmt.Sprintf from a value that is not a literal: a parameter, a call result, a member, or a variable assigned in the function (followed to its value when the query is a lone name), but not a constant or a name that reads as a table, column or sort key."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use placeholders and pass the values as arguments: db.Query(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1\", id) (? for MySQL and SQLite), or GORM's db.Where(\"name = ?\", name). Names of tables and columns cannot be bound, so check them against an allowlist.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** database/sql's Query, QueryRow, Exec and Prepare (and their Context forms), sqlx's Queryx, MustExec and NamedExec, and GORM's Raw, Exec, Where, Order, Group, Having and Joins run the SQL text they are given. A request value placed in that text can close a string and change the query. The finding is high when request data reaches the call inside one function (with a trace) and medium with low confidence when the text is built with + or fmt.Sprintf from a value that is not a literal: a parameter, a call result, a member, or a variable assigned in the function (followed to its value when the query is a lone name), but not a constant or a name that reads as a table, column or sort key.\n\n**Fix:** Use placeholders and pass the values as arguments: db.Query(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1\", id) (? for MySQL and SQLite), or GORM's db.Where(\"name = ?\", name). Names of tables and columns cannot be bound, so check them against an allowlist."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "go.xss.unescaped",
              "name": "GoXssUnescaped",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Request data marked as safe HTML, JavaScript or URL (template.HTML and its relatives)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "html/template escapes every value except those of the types template.HTML, template.JS, template.URL, template.HTMLAttr, template.CSS, template.JSStr and template.Srcset, which it trusts as already safe. Converting request data to one of them puts it into the page as markup or script (cross-site scripting). The finding appears when request data reaches the conversion inside one function (with a trace)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass plain strings to html/template and let it escape them; keep template.HTML for markup your own code built from constants, or sanitize user HTML first (for example with bluemonday).",
                "markdown": "**Why:** html/template escapes every value except those of the types template.HTML, template.JS, template.URL, template.HTMLAttr, template.CSS, template.JSStr and template.Srcset, which it trusts as already safe. Converting request data to one of them puts it into the page as markup or script (cross-site scripting). The finding appears when request data reaches the conversion inside one function (with a trace).\n\n**Fix:** Pass plain strings to html/template and let it escape them; keep template.HTML for markup your own code built from constants, or sanitize user HTML first (for example with bluemonday)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
