              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "high",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-295"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "java.sql.injection",
              "name": "JavaSqlInjection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL text built from request data (or built from a value that is not a literal), Java and Kotlin"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "JDBC's Statement.executeQuery, execute, executeUpdate and addBatch, Connection.prepareStatement and prepareCall (with text built before the call), JPA and Hibernate createQuery and createNativeQuery, Spring's JdbcTemplate query, queryForObject, queryForList and update, and Android's rawQuery and execSQL run the SQL text they are given. A request value placed in that text can close a string and change the query. The finding is high when request data reaches the call inside one method (with a trace) and medium with low confidence when the text is built with +, a StringBuilder, String.format or a Kotlin template from a value that is not a literal: a parameter, a call result, or a variable assigned in the method (followed to its value when the query is a lone name), but not a constant or a name that reads as a table, column or sort key."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use a PreparedStatement with ? placeholders and setString/setInt, JPA parameters (:name with setParameter), or JdbcTemplate's query(sql, args). Names of tables and columns cannot be bound, so check them against an allowlist.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** JDBC's Statement.executeQuery, execute, executeUpdate and addBatch, Connection.prepareStatement and prepareCall (with text built before the call), JPA and Hibernate createQuery and createNativeQuery, Spring's JdbcTemplate query, queryForObject, queryForList and update, and Android's rawQuery and execSQL run the SQL text they are given. A request value placed in that text can close a string and change the query. The finding is high when request data reaches the call inside one method (with a trace) and medium with low confidence when the text is built with +, a StringBuilder, String.format or a Kotlin template from a value that is not a literal: a parameter, a call result, or a variable assigned in the method (followed to its value when the query is a lone name), but not a constant or a name that reads as a table, column or sort key.\n\n**Fix:** Use a PreparedStatement with ? placeholders and setString/setInt, JPA parameters (:name with setParameter), or JdbcTemplate's query(sql, args). Names of tables and columns cannot be bound, so check them against an allowlist."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
