            {
              "id": "java.xxe.parser",
              "name": "JavaXxeParser",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "XML parser created without disabling DOCTYPEs or external entities, Java and Kotlin"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "DocumentBuilderFactory, SAXParserFactory, XMLInputFactory, TransformerFactory, SchemaFactory, XMLReaderFactory and dom4j's SAXReader or JDOM's SAXBuilder resolve DOCTYPE declarations and external entities by default. Parsing XML from outside can then read local files, reach internal URLs or exhaust memory (XML external entities). The finding appears where such a parser is created in a file that sets none of the protections: disallow-doctype-decl, the external-entity features, XMLConstants.FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING or ACCESS_EXTERNAL_DTD, IS_SUPPORTING_EXTERNAL_ENTITIES/SUPPORT_DTD false, or an entity resolver."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Turn DOCTYPEs off on the factory before using it: factory.setFeature(\"http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl\", true). Where DOCTYPEs are needed, turn off external-general-entities, external-parameter-entities and load-external-dtd, and set XMLConstants.ACCESS_EXTERNAL_DTD and ACCESS_EXTERNAL_SCHEMA to \"\". For StAX, set XMLInputFactory.SUPPORT_DTD and IS_SUPPORTING_EXTERNAL_ENTITIES to false.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** DocumentBuilderFactory, SAXParserFactory, XMLInputFactory, TransformerFactory, SchemaFactory, XMLReaderFactory and dom4j's SAXReader or JDOM's SAXBuilder resolve DOCTYPE declarations and external entities by default. Parsing XML from outside can then read local files, reach internal URLs or exhaust memory (XML external entities). The finding appears where such a parser is created in a file that sets none of the protections: disallow-doctype-decl, the external-entity features, XMLConstants.FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING or ACCESS_EXTERNAL_DTD, IS_SUPPORTING_EXTERNAL_ENTITIES/SUPPORT_DTD false, or an entity resolver.\n\n**Fix:** Turn DOCTYPEs off on the factory before using it: factory.setFeature(\"http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl\", true). Where DOCTYPEs are needed, turn off external-general-entities, external-parameter-entities and load-external-dtd, and set XMLConstants.ACCESS_EXTERNAL_DTD and ACCESS_EXTERNAL_SCHEMA to \"\". For StAX, set XMLInputFactory.SUPPORT_DTD and IS_SUPPORTING_EXTERNAL_ENTITIES to false."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-611"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "java.path.traversal",
              "name": "JavaPathTraversal",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "File path built from request data, Java and Kotlin"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "new File(), new FileInputStream(), new FileOutputStream(), new FileReader(), Paths.get(), Path.of() and the Files methods use the path they are given. A request value (or the name of an archive entry, zip slip) with '../' or an absolute path in it reaches files outside the intended directory."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Resolve the value against a fixed base directory, normalize it, and check the result still starts with the base: Path p = base.resolve(name).normalize(); if (!p.startsWith(base)) reject. For a file name, keep only Paths.get(name).getFileName() or FilenameUtils.getName().",
                "markdown": "**Why:** new File(), new FileInputStream(), new FileOutputStream(), new FileReader(), Paths.get(), Path.of() and the Files methods use the path they are given. A request value (or the name of an archive entry, zip slip) with '../' or an absolute path in it reaches files outside the intended directory.\n\n**Fix:** Resolve the value against a fixed base directory, normalize it, and check the result still starts with the base: Path p = base.resolve(name).normalize(); if (!p.startsWith(base)) reject. For a file name, keep only Paths.get(name).getFileName() or FilenameUtils.getName()."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "java.ssrf.request",
              "name": "JavaSsrfRequest",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Outbound request to a URL from request data, Java and Kotlin"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "new URL(), URI.create(), Apache HttpClient's new HttpGet()/HttpPost(), Spring's RestTemplate getForObject/postForObject/exchange, OkHttp's Request.Builder().url() and Jsoup.connect() point at whatever URL they are given. If the request chooses it, the server can be made to reach internal services or cloud metadata endpoints (server-side request forgery). Form-validation endpoints that test a user-supplied URL are a common case."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Allow only known schemes and hosts (compare the parsed URI's host with an allowlist), or map a request value to a fixed URL. When any host must be allowed, resolve it and refuse private, loopback and link-local addresses, and turn off automatic redirects. Check the permission of the user before a validation endpoint connects anywhere.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** new URL(), URI.create(), Apache HttpClient's new HttpGet()/HttpPost(), Spring's RestTemplate getForObject/postForObject/exchange, OkHttp's Request.Builder().url() and Jsoup.connect() point at whatever URL they are given. If the request chooses it, the server can be made to reach internal services or cloud metadata endpoints (server-side request forgery). Form-validation endpoints that test a user-supplied URL are a common case.\n\n**Fix:** Allow only known schemes and hosts (compare the parsed URI's host with an allowlist), or map a request value to a fixed URL. When any host must be allowed, resolve it and refuse private, loopback and link-local addresses, and turn off automatic redirects. Check the permission of the user before a validation endpoint connects anywhere."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "java.xss.response",
              "name": "JavaXssResponse",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Request data written into the response unescaped, Java and Kotlin"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "response.getWriter().write/print/println, getOutputStream().print and a JSP-style out.print write their argument into the page as is, and Ktor's call.respondText with an HTML content type does the same. A request value in it becomes markup the browser runs as script (reflected cross-site scripting)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Render through a template engine that escapes (Thymeleaf, JSP with <c:out>, Jelly with escape-by-default), or escape for the context with the OWASP Java Encoder (Encode.forHtml) or Spring's HtmlUtils.htmlEscape, and send plain text as text/plain.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** response.getWriter().write/print/println, getOutputStream().print and a JSP-style out.print write their argument into the page as is, and Ktor's call.respondText with an HTML content type does the same. A request value in it becomes markup the browser runs as script (reflected cross-site scripting).\n\n**Fix:** Render through a template engine that escapes (Thymeleaf, JSP with <c:out>, Jelly with escape-by-default), or escape for the context with the OWASP Java Encoder (Encode.forHtml) or Spring's HtmlUtils.htmlEscape, and send plain text as text/plain."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cs.sql.injection",
              "name": "CsSqlInjection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL text built from request data (or built from a value that is not a literal), C#"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "new SqlCommand(), SqlDataAdapter and the other ADO.NET commands (OleDb, Odbc, MySQL, Npgsql, SQLite, Oracle), CommandText, Entity Framework's ExecuteSqlRaw, FromSqlRaw and ExecuteSqlCommand, and Dapper's Query and Execute run the SQL text they are given. A request value placed in that text can close a string and change the query. The finding is high when request data (Request.Query, Request.Form, a [FromQuery]/[FromForm]/[FromRoute]/[FromBody] parameter or a string parameter of a controller action) reaches the call inside one method (with a trace), and medium with low confidence when the text is built with +, string.Format or an interpolated string from a value that is not a literal (a parameter, a call result or a local variable; not a constant or a table, column or sort-key name)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use parameters: new SqlCommand(\"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Id = @id\", conn) with cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@id\", id), Dapper's anonymous parameter object, or Entity Framework's FromSql/FromSqlInterpolated and ExecuteSql, which turn interpolated values into parameters. Never pass an interpolated string to the *Raw methods.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** new SqlCommand(), SqlDataAdapter and the other ADO.NET commands (OleDb, Odbc, MySQL, Npgsql, SQLite, Oracle), CommandText, Entity Framework's ExecuteSqlRaw, FromSqlRaw and ExecuteSqlCommand, and Dapper's Query and Execute run the SQL text they are given. A request value placed in that text can close a string and change the query. The finding is high when request data (Request.Query, Request.Form, a [FromQuery]/[FromForm]/[FromRoute]/[FromBody] parameter or a string parameter of a controller action) reaches the call inside one method (with a trace), and medium with low confidence when the text is built with +, string.Format or an interpolated string from a value that is not a literal (a parameter, a call result or a local variable; not a constant or a table, column or sort-key name).\n\n**Fix:** Use parameters: new SqlCommand(\"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Id = @id\", conn) with cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@id\", id), Dapper's anonymous parameter object, or Entity Framework's FromSql/FromSqlInterpolated and ExecuteSql, which turn interpolated values into parameters. Never pass an interpolated string to the *Raw methods."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
