            },
            {
              "id": "rb.sql.injection",
              "name": "RbSqlInjection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL text built from request data (or built from a value that is not a literal), Ruby on Rails"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Active Record's find_by_sql, where, order, pluck, select, joins, group, having, from, exists?, delete_all, update_all and the connection's execute, exec_query and select_* put a string argument into the SQL as is. params, cookies or request data interpolated into it ('#{params[:id]}') or concatenated can close a string and change the query; order(params[:sort]) is a classic case. The finding is high when request data reaches the call inside one method (with a trace) and medium with low confidence when the text is interpolated or concatenated from a value that is not a literal (a parameter, a call result, an instance variable or a local variable; not a constant or a table, column or sort-key name). Hash conditions (where(name: value)) and placeholders (where('name = ?', value)) are not reported."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass values separately: where(name: value), where('name = ?', value), where('name = :name', name: value), or sanitize_sql_array. For order and column names, map the request value to an allowlist of columns and directions.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Active Record's find_by_sql, where, order, pluck, select, joins, group, having, from, exists?, delete_all, update_all and the connection's execute, exec_query and select_* put a string argument into the SQL as is. params, cookies or request data interpolated into it ('#{params[:id]}') or concatenated can close a string and change the query; order(params[:sort]) is a classic case. The finding is high when request data reaches the call inside one method (with a trace) and medium with low confidence when the text is interpolated or concatenated from a value that is not a literal (a parameter, a call result, an instance variable or a local variable; not a constant or a table, column or sort-key name). Hash conditions (where(name: value)) and placeholders (where('name = ?', value)) are not reported.\n\n**Fix:** Pass values separately: where(name: value), where('name = ?', value), where('name = :name', name: value), or sanitize_sql_array. For order and column names, map the request value to an allowlist of columns and directions."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "rb.xss.html_safe",
              "name": "RbXssHtmlSafe",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "html_safe, raw or <%== %> of a value that is not a literal, Ruby on Rails"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Rails escapes every value printed in a view unless it is marked safe. html_safe, raw() and ERB's <%== %> mark it safe, so markup in it runs as script (cross-site scripting). The finding is high when the value comes from params, cookies or request (with a trace in Ruby files), and low with low confidence when it is only not a literal."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Print the value with <%= %> and let Rails escape it. For user-supplied HTML that must stay HTML, use sanitize(value) with an allowlist of tags; build markup with content_tag and tag helpers, which escape their contents.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** Rails escapes every value printed in a view unless it is marked safe. html_safe, raw() and ERB's <%== %> mark it safe, so markup in it runs as script (cross-site scripting). The finding is high when the value comes from params, cookies or request (with a trace in Ruby files), and low with low confidence when it is only not a literal.\n\n**Fix:** Print the value with <%= %> and let Rails escape it. For user-supplied HTML that must stay HTML, use sanitize(value) with an allowlist of tags; build markup with content_tag and tag helpers, which escape their contents."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "rb.reflection.constantize",
              "name": "RbReflectionConstantize",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "constantize or const_get of request data, Ruby on Rails"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "constantize, safe_constantize and Object.const_get turn a string into whatever class or module it names. If the request chooses the name, the code instantiates or calls methods on classes the developer never meant to expose, which can lead to code execution or data exposure (unsafe reflection)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Map the request value to a class through an explicit allowlist, for example { 'user' => User, 'admin' => Admin }.fetch(params[:type]).",
                "markdown": "**Why:** constantize, safe_constantize and Object.const_get turn a string into whatever class or module it names. If the request chooses the name, the code instantiates or calls methods on classes the developer never meant to expose, which can lead to code execution or data exposure (unsafe reflection).\n\n**Fix:** Map the request value to a class through an explicit allowlist, for example { 'user' => User, 'admin' => Admin }.fetch(params[:type])."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.0",
                "precision": "medium",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-470"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "rb.reflection.send",
              "name": "RbReflectionSend",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "send, public_send or try with a method name from request data, Ruby"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "send, __send__, public_send and try call the method they are given by name. If the request chooses the name, it can call any method of the object, including private ones with send (for example destroy, update or system through Kernel)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Check the name against an allowlist of permitted methods first (ALLOWED.include?(name)), or dispatch through a case statement or a hash of lambdas.",
                "markdown": "**Why:** send, __send__, public_send and try call the method they are given by name. If the request chooses the name, it can call any method of the object, including private ones with send (for example destroy, update or system through Kernel).\n\n**Fix:** Check the name against an allowlist of permitted methods first (ALLOWED.include?(name)), or dispatch through a case statement or a hash of lambdas."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/Ruthvik-Bandari/kestrix/blob/main/docs/modules/code.md#rules",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
